1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,600
Brain Tricks 10 Mind Bending Illusions Your Brain

2
00:00:03,500 --> 00:00:09,040
Plays on You Introduction What if I told

3
00:00:08,940 --> 00:00:11,140
you that you cannot always trust what you

4
00:00:11,040 --> 00:00:11,500
see,

5
00:00:11,860 --> 00:00:12,180
hear,

6
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:13,420
or even remember?

7
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:16,360
Your brain is one of the most powerful

8
00:00:16,260 --> 00:00:17,860
organs in the human body.

9
00:00:18,400 --> 00:00:20,340
It processes millions of signals,

10
00:00:20,660 --> 00:00:22,420
creates your perception of reality,

11
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:25,080
and helps you understand the world around you.

12
00:00:25,580 --> 00:00:26,960
But here's something fascinating.

13
00:00:27,700 --> 00:00:30,120
Your brain doesn't simply record reality,

14
00:00:30,020 --> 00:00:31,600
it interprets it.

15
00:00:32,080 --> 00:00:32,780
And sometimes,

16
00:00:33,160 --> 00:00:35,720
that interpretation can be completely misleading.

17
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:39,400
From remembering things that never happened to feeling

18
00:00:39,300 --> 00:00:41,240
your phone vibrate when it hasn't,

19
00:00:41,620 --> 00:00:44,740
your brain performs some truly extraordinary tricks.

20
00:00:45,580 --> 00:00:45,960
Today,

21
00:00:46,220 --> 00:00:49,740
we're exploring 10 fascinating illusions your brain creates

22
00:00:49,640 --> 00:00:51,580
without you even realizing it.

23
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:54,460
And number 10 might make you question some

24
00:00:54,360 --> 00:00:55,280
of your own memories.

25
00:00:57,180 --> 00:00:57,600
1.

26
00:00:58,420 --> 00:00:59,000
Déjà vu.

27
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:01,880
The feeling that you've lived this moment before.

28
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:04,740
Have you ever entered a room,

29
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:05,940
heard someone speak,

30
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:08,740
or experienced a completely ordinary moment,

31
00:01:09,020 --> 00:01:11,700
and suddenly felt like you've experienced exactly the

32
00:01:11,600 --> 00:01:12,580
same thing before?

33
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:16,320
This strange sensation is called Déjà vu.

34
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:19,360
The interesting part is that you usually know

35
00:01:19,260 --> 00:01:21,440
the moment is happening for the first time,

36
00:01:21,720 --> 00:01:23,660
yet it feels strangely familiar.

37
00:01:24,480 --> 00:01:27,580
Scientists believe Déjà vu may involve a temporary

38
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:31,040
mismatch between the brain systems responsible for familiarity

39
00:01:30,940 --> 00:01:31,980
and memory.

40
00:01:32,520 --> 00:01:33,580
In simple terms,

41
00:01:34,000 --> 00:01:36,480
your brain may mistakenly give a new experience

42
00:01:36,380 --> 00:01:38,200
the feeling of an old memory.

43
00:01:38,860 --> 00:01:41,640
It's almost like your brain recognizes something before

44
00:01:41,540 --> 00:01:42,840
it can explain why.

45
00:01:44,860 --> 00:01:45,240
2.

46
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:46,820
Phantom vibrations.

47
00:01:47,460 --> 00:01:49,340
Why you feel your phone ring when it

48
00:01:49,240 --> 00:01:49,800
doesn't.

49
00:01:51,040 --> 00:01:53,660
Imagine reaching into your pocket because you feel

50
00:01:53,560 --> 00:01:54,540
your phone vibrating.

51
00:01:55,120 --> 00:01:55,900
You check it.

52
00:01:56,160 --> 00:01:56,780
No messages,

53
00:01:57,160 --> 00:01:57,760
no calls,

54
00:01:58,160 --> 00:01:58,580
nothing.

55
00:01:59,280 --> 00:02:01,900
This is known as Phantom vibration syndrome.

56
00:02:02,520 --> 00:02:05,280
It happens when your brain interprets ordinary physical

57
00:02:05,180 --> 00:02:05,940
sensations,

58
00:02:06,320 --> 00:02:08,840
such as muscle movements or clothing touching your

59
00:02:08,740 --> 00:02:10,520
skin as a phone notification.

60
00:02:11,400 --> 00:02:13,980
If you frequently expect messages or calls,

61
00:02:14,360 --> 00:02:17,120
your attention may become especially sensitive to these

62
00:02:17,020 --> 00:02:17,640
sensations.

63
00:02:18,280 --> 00:02:20,900
Your brain is constantly trying to predict what

64
00:02:20,800 --> 00:02:21,880
might happen next,

65
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:24,720
and sometimes it gets the prediction wrong.

66
00:02:26,420 --> 00:02:27,160
3.

67
00:02:27,660 --> 00:02:28,720
Optical illusions.

68
00:02:29,300 --> 00:02:31,060
Why your eyes can fool you.

69
00:02:32,360 --> 00:02:33,920
Look at an optical illusion,

70
00:02:34,160 --> 00:02:36,200
and you might see movement where nothing is

71
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:36,500
moving.

72
00:02:37,040 --> 00:02:39,560
You might even see two identical colors as

73
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:40,940
completely different shades.

74
00:02:41,540 --> 00:02:42,640
Why does this happen?

75
00:02:43,220 --> 00:02:45,560
Because seeing isn't just about your eyes.

76
00:02:45,860 --> 00:02:47,240
Your eyes collect light,

77
00:02:47,140 --> 00:02:50,780
but your brain interprets that information using contrast,

78
00:02:51,280 --> 00:02:51,680
lighting,

79
00:02:52,180 --> 00:02:52,580
shadows,

80
00:02:53,040 --> 00:02:53,500
patterns,

81
00:02:53,840 --> 00:02:55,200
and previous experiences.

82
00:02:55,920 --> 00:02:58,260
That's why the same color can appear lighter

83
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:00,320
or darker depending on its surroundings.

84
00:03:01,140 --> 00:03:03,320
Your brain doesn't simply show you the world

85
00:03:03,220 --> 00:03:04,000
as it is.

86
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:07,060
It builds an interpretation of what it thinks

87
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:07,880
you're looking at.

88
00:03:09,540 --> 00:03:09,820
4.

89
00:03:10,360 --> 00:03:11,280
Time distortion.

90
00:03:11,900 --> 00:03:14,180
Why time sometimes feels slower?

91
00:03:15,340 --> 00:03:17,840
Have you ever experienced a frightening moment that

92
00:03:17,740 --> 00:03:20,140
seemed to last much longer than it actually

93
00:03:20,040 --> 00:03:20,480
did?

94
00:03:21,020 --> 00:03:23,120
Or noticed how an hour with friends can

95
00:03:23,020 --> 00:03:24,480
feel like just a few minutes?

96
00:03:25,040 --> 00:03:28,160
Time perception isn't controlled by a single internal

97
00:03:28,060 --> 00:03:28,580
clock.

98
00:03:29,180 --> 00:03:30,400
It depends on attention,

99
00:03:30,860 --> 00:03:31,320
emotions,

100
00:03:31,800 --> 00:03:32,240
memory,

101
00:03:32,600 --> 00:03:35,020
and how much information your brain is processing.

102
00:03:35,660 --> 00:03:38,600
During frightening or highly emotional situations,

103
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:41,540
your brain may pay closer attention to details.

104
00:03:42,320 --> 00:03:44,320
When you remember the experience afterward,

105
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:47,280
those details can make the event seem longer.

106
00:03:47,840 --> 00:03:50,720
But that doesn't necessarily mean your brain literally

107
00:03:50,620 --> 00:03:51,960
slowed down time.

108
00:03:52,520 --> 00:03:55,040
It means your experience of time changed.

109
00:03:56,740 --> 00:03:57,140
5.

110
00:03:57,880 --> 00:03:58,620
False memories.

111
00:03:59,140 --> 00:04:01,520
Can you remember something that never happened?

112
00:04:02,600 --> 00:04:04,340
Here's something truly surprising.

113
00:04:05,080 --> 00:04:07,720
You can confidently remember an event that never

114
00:04:07,620 --> 00:04:10,020
happened exactly the way you remember it.

115
00:04:10,420 --> 00:04:12,760
Human memory isn't like a video recording.

116
00:04:13,360 --> 00:04:14,980
Every time you recall a memory,

117
00:04:15,380 --> 00:04:18,080
your brain reconstructs it using stored information,

118
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:19,040
emotions,

119
00:04:19,460 --> 00:04:21,080
and sometimes new details.

120
00:04:21,820 --> 00:04:23,740
This process can introduce mistakes.

121
00:04:24,460 --> 00:04:25,100
For example,

122
00:04:25,500 --> 00:04:28,160
someone might repeatedly describe an event differently,

123
00:04:28,680 --> 00:04:29,600
and over time,

124
00:04:29,900 --> 00:04:32,320
you may begin to remember those details as

125
00:04:32,220 --> 00:04:33,900
if you experienced them yourself.

126
00:04:34,540 --> 00:04:36,920
That's why two people can remember the same

127
00:04:36,820 --> 00:04:38,920
event in completely different ways,

128
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:42,300
and why confidence doesn't always mean accuracy.

129
00:04:44,120 --> 00:04:44,480
6.

130
00:04:45,040 --> 00:04:46,120
The placebo effect.

131
00:04:46,640 --> 00:04:49,180
How expectations can change what you feel?

132
00:04:50,340 --> 00:04:52,740
Can believing something will help you actually make

133
00:04:52,640 --> 00:04:53,480
you feel better?

134
00:04:53,960 --> 00:04:54,560
Sometimes,

135
00:04:54,920 --> 00:04:55,280
yes.

136
00:04:55,860 --> 00:04:57,920
This is known as the placebo effect.

137
00:04:58,500 --> 00:04:59,600
In medical research,

138
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:02,340
a placebo is a treatment that doesn't contain

139
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:04,160
the active ingredient being tested.

140
00:05:04,520 --> 00:05:07,720
Yet some people receiving a placebo report improvements

141
00:05:07,620 --> 00:05:08,520
in their symptoms.

142
00:05:09,200 --> 00:05:09,560
Why?

143
00:05:10,160 --> 00:05:13,360
Because expectations can influence how the brain processes

144
00:05:13,260 --> 00:05:13,920
pain,

145
00:05:14,280 --> 00:05:14,760
discomfort,

146
00:05:15,120 --> 00:05:16,160
and other sensations.

147
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:19,880
Research has shown that the brain's own pain-regulating

148
00:05:19,780 --> 00:05:22,080
systems can play a role in this effect.

149
00:05:22,660 --> 00:05:23,140
However,

150
00:05:23,340 --> 00:05:26,320
the placebo effect doesn't mean every illness can

151
00:05:26,220 --> 00:05:27,880
be cured by positive thinking.

152
00:05:28,340 --> 00:05:32,000
It shows just how strongly expectations can influence

153
00:05:31,900 --> 00:05:33,400
our experience of the body.

154
00:05:35,140 --> 00:05:35,480
7.

155
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:36,880
Contagious itching.

156
00:05:37,440 --> 00:05:39,960
Why seeing someone scratch can make you itch?

157
00:05:41,020 --> 00:05:43,240
Have you ever watched someone scratch their arm

158
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:45,020
and suddenly felt an itch yourself?

159
00:05:45,520 --> 00:05:46,600
It sounds strange,

160
00:05:46,780 --> 00:05:47,640
but it happens.

161
00:05:48,380 --> 00:05:50,900
Our brains are highly responsive to what other

162
00:05:50,800 --> 00:05:51,500
people do.

163
00:05:52,120 --> 00:05:55,200
Seeing someone scratch can activate attention and brain

164
00:05:55,100 --> 00:05:57,200
processes associated with itching.

165
00:05:57,640 --> 00:06:00,340
This may increase your awareness of sensations on

166
00:06:00,240 --> 00:06:01,020
your own skin.

167
00:06:01,440 --> 00:06:03,560
The same kind of response can happen when

168
00:06:03,460 --> 00:06:05,300
you hear someone talking about itching.

169
00:06:05,860 --> 00:06:08,340
Your brain doesn't need an actual insect bite

170
00:06:08,240 --> 00:06:09,720
to make you notice an itch.

171
00:06:10,000 --> 00:06:10,580
Sometimes,

172
00:06:10,920 --> 00:06:13,520
simply seeing or thinking about it is enough.

173
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:15,600
8.

174
00:06:16,160 --> 00:06:17,220
Auditory illusions.

175
00:06:17,880 --> 00:06:20,300
Why you sometimes hear things that aren't there?

176
00:06:21,460 --> 00:06:23,400
Have you ever thought someone called your name

177
00:06:23,300 --> 00:06:25,420
only to discover that nobody did?

178
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:28,540
This can happen because your brain doesn't passively

179
00:06:28,440 --> 00:06:29,440
receive sounds.

180
00:06:29,340 --> 00:06:33,560
It constantly uses context and expectations to interpret

181
00:06:33,460 --> 00:06:34,220
what it hears.

182
00:06:34,880 --> 00:06:35,620
When you're tired,

183
00:06:35,980 --> 00:06:36,520
distracted,

184
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:38,480
or expecting someone to call you,

185
00:06:38,780 --> 00:06:41,320
an unclear sound can be interpreted as a

186
00:06:41,220 --> 00:06:42,140
familiar voice.

187
00:06:42,720 --> 00:06:45,760
Another interesting example is Auditory Paridolia.

188
00:06:46,320 --> 00:06:49,340
This happens when your brain recognizes meaningful sounds

189
00:06:49,240 --> 00:06:50,960
or patterns in random noise.

190
00:06:51,500 --> 00:06:52,140
For example,

191
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:54,240
you might hear what sounds like a voice

192
00:06:54,140 --> 00:06:56,280
in a fan or a strange noise in

193
00:06:56,180 --> 00:06:56,820
the background.

194
00:06:57,440 --> 00:06:59,700
Your brain is always searching for patterns,

195
00:06:59,940 --> 00:07:01,920
even when none are actually present.

196
00:07:03,720 --> 00:07:04,080
9.

197
00:07:04,740 --> 00:07:05,900
Inattentional blindness.

198
00:07:06,440 --> 00:07:08,420
How you can miss something right in front

199
00:07:08,320 --> 00:07:08,780
of you.

200
00:07:09,960 --> 00:07:13,160
Imagine looking directly at something without actually noticing

201
00:07:13,060 --> 00:07:13,500
it.

202
00:07:13,940 --> 00:07:14,860
Sounds impossible,

203
00:07:14,960 --> 00:07:15,240
right?

204
00:07:15,700 --> 00:07:18,540
But this is a real psychological phenomenon called

205
00:07:18,440 --> 00:07:19,880
inattentional blindness.

206
00:07:20,480 --> 00:07:22,560
When your attention is focused on a specific

207
00:07:22,460 --> 00:07:23,120
task,

208
00:07:23,380 --> 00:07:26,660
you can completely miss unexpected objects or events

209
00:07:26,560 --> 00:07:27,700
in your field of vision.

210
00:07:28,260 --> 00:07:31,220
One famous experiment asked people to count basketball

211
00:07:31,120 --> 00:07:32,280
passes in a video.

212
00:07:32,880 --> 00:07:35,300
Many participants failed to notice a person in

213
00:07:35,200 --> 00:07:38,060
a gorilla costume walking directly through the scene.

214
00:07:38,560 --> 00:07:39,640
Their eyes were open,

215
00:07:39,800 --> 00:07:41,640
but their attention was somewhere else.

216
00:07:42,220 --> 00:07:43,740
This reveals something important.

217
00:07:44,320 --> 00:07:47,420
Seeing something and consciously noticing it are not

218
00:07:47,320 --> 00:07:48,180
the same thing.

219
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:50,280
10.

220
00:07:50,940 --> 00:07:51,780
Dream confusion.

221
00:07:51,680 --> 00:07:54,700
When your brain mixes dreams with reality,

222
00:07:56,040 --> 00:07:57,600
have you ever woken up and needed a

223
00:07:57,500 --> 00:07:59,860
few seconds to figure out whether something actually

224
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:01,720
happened or whether you dreamed it?

225
00:08:02,200 --> 00:08:05,060
Dreams can sometimes feel incredibly realistic.

226
00:08:05,720 --> 00:08:06,400
During sleep,

227
00:08:06,720 --> 00:08:09,160
the brain creates experiences using memories,

228
00:08:09,620 --> 00:08:10,040
emotions,

229
00:08:10,440 --> 00:08:11,060
and imagination.

230
00:08:11,540 --> 00:08:12,460
When you wake up,

231
00:08:12,620 --> 00:08:13,760
especially suddenly,

232
00:08:14,240 --> 00:08:16,140
it can take a moment to organize those

233
00:08:16,040 --> 00:08:19,200
experiences and distinguish them from real memories.

234
00:08:19,900 --> 00:08:20,400
Sometimes,

235
00:08:20,900 --> 00:08:23,720
details from a dream can remain vivid even

236
00:08:23,620 --> 00:08:25,420
after you know it wasn't real.

237
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:28,780
This is another reminder that memory and perception

238
00:08:28,680 --> 00:08:31,240
are not perfect recordings of reality.

239
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:33,900
Your brain is constantly building,

240
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:34,760
interpreting,

241
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:37,180
and reconstructing your experiences.

242
00:08:39,040 --> 00:08:39,360
10.

243
00:08:39,260 --> 00:08:39,640
Conclusion.

244
00:08:40,800 --> 00:08:41,320
So,

245
00:08:41,620 --> 00:08:42,380
what have we learned?

246
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:45,880
Your brain can make new experiences feel familiar,

247
00:08:45,780 --> 00:08:48,800
create sensations that aren't actually happening,

248
00:08:49,200 --> 00:08:50,980
distort your perception of time,

249
00:08:51,200 --> 00:08:53,300
and even change the way you remember the

250
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:53,680
past.

251
00:08:54,180 --> 00:08:56,160
But these aren't signs that your brain is

252
00:08:56,060 --> 00:08:56,600
broken.

253
00:08:57,060 --> 00:09:00,280
They're consequences of a complex system designed to

254
00:09:00,180 --> 00:09:03,360
process information quickly and help you navigate the

255
00:09:03,260 --> 00:09:03,660
world.

256
00:09:04,280 --> 00:09:06,700
Your brain doesn't simply show you reality.

257
00:09:07,140 --> 00:09:09,460
It builds the version of reality that you

258
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:10,120
experience.

259
00:09:10,780 --> 00:09:13,420
And perhaps the most fascinating question is this.

260
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:16,020
How much of what you believe about the

261
00:09:15,920 --> 00:09:18,520
world is based on what actually happened,

262
00:09:18,880 --> 00:09:20,420
and how much is based on the way

263
00:09:20,320 --> 00:09:21,940
your brain interpreted it?

264
00:09:22,480 --> 00:09:24,940
If you enjoyed discovering these hidden tricks of

265
00:09:24,840 --> 00:09:25,700
the human brain,

266
00:09:26,040 --> 00:09:29,220
subscribe to Body Hacks for more fascinating science

267
00:09:29,120 --> 00:09:30,960
about the human body and mind.

268
00:09:31,480 --> 00:09:32,580
And tell us in the comments,

269
00:09:33,200 --> 00:09:35,500
which of these brain tricks have you experienced

270
00:09:35,400 --> 00:09:36,020
yourself?
